    '--- name: flint-chart description: "Use when the user wants to visualize data — from \'which chart should I use?\' to \'render this\'. Helps pick the right chart from the analytical question (comparison / trend / distribution / relationship / proportion / flow / KPI), then authors a ChartAssemblyInput and renders via the flint-chart-mcp server (Vega-Lite / ECharts / Chart.js). Transform data before Flint; style tweaks after Flint." lastReviewed: 2026-11-05 --- # Flint chart authoring and rendering Reviewed runtime: `flint-chart-mcp@1.0.1` with exact `flint-chart@1.0.1`. Flint owns its language; Alex owns analytical framing, evidence, consent, and render verification. Do not copy a static schema/type catalog into this skill. ## Load the installed language first Read `flint://agent-skill` or call `get_flint_skill({ "skill": "chart" })`. For reader behavior, chart-state updates, or linked views, load and follow [flint-interactions](../flint-interactions/SKILL.md) and its selected native `interaction` or `board` skill/resource. Use `list_chart_types`, `list_interaction_presets`, and `list_themes` to verify actual capabilities before promising them. Report an unavailable resource/tool instead of inventing grammar. The [language and backend boundary](references/flint-language-reference.md) provides orientation, not a replacement for version-matched schemas. For selection, load only the relevant [question-to-chart guidance](references/chart-selection.md). ## Step 0: Frame the analytical question When the user has not supplied a complete spec, load [chart-big-idea](../chart-big-idea/SKILL.md). Reuse an existing Chart Brief and explicit treatment rather than asking the same questions again. A diagnostic chart or a small style iteration can use the owner\'s fast path. Check the source data, actual values, population, period, units, denominator, and grain. Separate totals from their parts. Compute material shares/ratios using a tool; if computation is blocked, label the claim unverified rather than reporting prose arithmetic as evidence. ## Step 1: Choose an exact supported chartType Use the exact case-sensitive name from `list_chart_types` for the chosen backend, for example `Stacked Bar Chart`, not a snake_case family hint. The [selection reference](references/chart-selection.md) covers the analytical question, anti-patterns, and substitutions; the runtime catalog settles support. Do not treat arbitrary layering suggestions as standalone registered chart types. ## Step 2: Bind data and encodings Author `ChartAssemblyInput`, not backend-native output. Use actual field names and registered semantic types; `Category` and `Quantity` are safe defaults when their meaning fits. Keep the same data/semantic truth layer for alternate treatments. Transform data before Flint: do not invent a transformation API. For small synthetic/prepared tables, use `data.values`. `data.url` supports local JSON/CSV/TSV when the server permits file references, not remote URL fetch. Use a real parser for quoted CSV. Do not manually reserialize a large dataset. Preserve the claim in `chart_spec.title`; use `subtitle` for population, period, measure, and units. Do not invent missing metadata. ## Step 3: Theme, interaction, and presentation For custom themes, load [flint-theme](../flint-theme/SKILL.md) and its version-matched grammar. In the exposed three-backend MCP workflow, ThemeSpec applies to Vega-Lite; do not claim ECharts/Chart.js parity. The library\'s additional backends are separate from the MCP backend enum. When the request or exploratory session calls for behavior/state/linking, load and follow [flint-interactions](../flint-interactions/SKILL.md) to choose the smallest compatible workflow and define gesture/reset acceptance. It owns `interaction_spec` versus `updates` versus linking decisions; this skill keeps the base chart/data/display contract. Static-only requests do not acquire interactions. Use `validate_board` before displaying a board. ## Step 4: Validate, then display - For interactive Vega-Lite with App UI support: call `validate_chart` first, resolve errors/warnings, then `show({ "chart": input })` with the whole input. The server retains no chart state between calls. A later update requires sending the revised complete input; earlier cards remain as they were. - For a board: validate, then `show({ "board": boardInput })`. - Use `render_chart` for requested static output or a host without App UI: Vega-Lite/ECharts support SVG and PNG; Chart.js supports PNG only. - Use `compile_chart` for backend-native JSON. Do not send edited backend JSON back to Flint as a portable input. If narrow post-compile styling is needed, render it with that backend and verify the actual result. ## Step 5: Verify the output, not merely the spec Load and follow [render-verify](../render-verify/SKILL.md) after rendering, especially after a backend edit. Inspect console errors, marks, title/labels, units, data values, clipping, and the intended claim. A valid spec can still render the wrong story. Use native browser tools first, then the registered fallback for unavailable scenarios; report any verification gap. For publication-specific formatting, load the [publication preset](references/publication-preset.md), and the applicable [print SVG](../print-svg-style-guide/SKILL.md) or [figure generator](../figure-generator/SKILL.md) contract. ## Runtime and project integration Use the plugin\'s direct Node launcher and reviewed exact pins. Never substitute an unpinned npx server, change npm registry/policy, or install dependencies silently. Missing state routes to [setup-dependencies](../setup-dependencies/SKILL.md). Only add `flint-chart@1.0.1` and approved renderer peers to a user\'s project when project integration is explicitly requested and approved. Report the chosen analytical treatment, strongest rejected alternative, actual backend/tool path, warnings, and observed verification. Do not claim a native feature, model, font, or App UI was available merely because documentation names it. ## Would Revise If Revise when installed tool/resource schemas, chart names, display semantics, backend support, theme behavior, or a reproduced visual failure contradict this contract. The exact runtime catalog outranks stale copied examples. '
    
